.TH PRIVACYFOX-APPLY 1 "September 2026" "privacyfox 0.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
privacyfox-apply \- apply or revert PrivacyFox's hardened profile on your LibreWolf install
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B privacyfox-apply
.br
.B privacyfox-apply
.B \-\-revert
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B privacyfox-apply
finishes the setup that the
.B privacyfox
package's install step can't safely do itself. Installing the package
already drops
.I distribution/policies.json
into LibreWolf's system install directory \- that part runs as root,
during
.BR "apt install" ,
and needs no further action.

What it can't do at install time is touch your personal LibreWolf
profile: Debian packaging rules don't allow a
.B postinst
maintainer script (which runs as root, with no specific user session)
to write into a user's home directory. That's the one remaining piece
.B privacyfox-apply
does, run once by you, as yourself, with no
.BR sudo .

Specifically, each run:
.IP \(bu 2
Refuses to proceed if LibreWolf is currently running, since LibreWolf
rewrites
.I user.js
and
.I prefs.js
on exit and would silently overwrite what this command just wrote.
.IP \(bu 2
Locates your default LibreWolf profile via
.IR profiles.ini ,
checking the legacy
.I ~/.librewolf
path first and falling back to the XDG
.I ~/.config/librewolf/librewolf
path only if the legacy one doesn't already exist \- matching
LibreWolf's own real compatibility rule. If none exists yet \-
LibreWolf has never been launched \- briefly launches it headless just
long enough to create one, then closes it again. No manual "open it
once yourself" step needed first.
.IP \(bu 2
Backs up any existing
.I user.js
in that profile to a timestamped
.I user.js.bak.<timestamp>
file before overwriting it.
.IP \(bu 2
Copies the hardening file
.RI ( PrivacyFox.js ,
shipped by the package at
.IR /usr/share/privacyfox/PrivacyFox.js )
into the profile as
.IR user.js .
This file is a real diff against LibreWolf's own actual defaults, not
Arkenfox/Betterfox applied blindly on top of them \- see the file's
own header for the methodology.
.IP \(bu 2
Copies
.I userContent.css
into the profile's
.I chrome/
directory, if the package ships one \- a small cosmetic cleanup,
skipped silently if absent.
.IP \(bu 2
On success, launches LibreWolf for real, so you see the hardened
result immediately.
.SH THE \-\-REVERT FLAG
.B "privacyfox-apply --revert"
undoes the profile side of things:
.I user.js
and
.I userContent.css
are restored from the newest pre-PrivacyFox backup found in the
profile, or removed outright if no backup exists (meaning the profile
had neither file before PrivacyFox ever touched it). LibreWolf is
then launched again so you can confirm stock behavior directly.
.PP
This is the counterpart to
.BR "sudo apt purge privacyfox" ,
which only undoes the system-wide side (the policy file and its
force-installed extensions) \- it has no access to your profile at
all, the same restriction that keeps the initial apply step out of
.B postinst
in the first place. Run
.B "privacyfox-apply --revert"
.I before
.BR "sudo apt purge privacyfox" ,
not after: purge removes this very command along with the rest of the
package, so reversing the order leaves the profile stuck hardened
with no way to revert it short of restoring a manual backup.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Applied successfully.
.TP
.B 1
LibreWolf was running, no LibreWolf profile could be found or
created, or the
.B privacyfox
package's files weren't present at
.IR /usr/share/privacyfox/ .
The error message printed explains which.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/privacyfox/PrivacyFox.js
The hardening file this command copies into your profile.
.TP
.I /usr/share/privacyfox/userContent.css
The optional cosmetic cleanup file, if shipped.
.TP
.I ~/.librewolf/profiles.ini
(or
.I ~/.config/librewolf/librewolf/profiles.ini
on newer, never-legacy installs) Read to find your default profile
directory. Not written to.
.TP
.I <profile>/user.js
Overwritten with the content of
.I PrivacyFox.js
each run, after a timestamped backup.
.TP
.I <profile>/user.js.bak.<timestamp>
.RI "and " <profile>/chrome/userContent.css.bak.<timestamp>
Backups created before each overwrite. Consumed (renamed back, not
copied) by
.BR "--revert" ,
newest first.
.SH NOTES
Safe to re-run at any time, including after
.B "apt upgrade privacyfox"
ships an updated
.IR PrivacyFox.js .
Each run simply refreshes
.I user.js
from whatever the currently installed package contains, backing up
the previous version first.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR librewolf (1),
.I policies.json
at
.IR /etc/librewolf/policies/policies.json ,
installed by the
.B privacyfox
package itself, and removed again on
.BR "apt purge privacyfox" .
.PP
Full source, credits, and documentation:
.UR https://github.com/rjc3rd/PrivacyFox
.UE
